Zig has rapidly become one of my favorite programming languages.  From first
use, I’ve found it to be simple, powerful, and elegant. 

# In Praise of Zig

One of my favorite things about Zig is its relatively small surface area.
Coming from languages like rust and C++, Zig is **simple**.  It took me maybe a
weekend to pick up, and after I got over such particularities as array typing
and comptime, there just wasn’t that more to understand.  Where complexities
exist, they usually reflect practical decisions around interacting with C and
assembly.  The language reference is short and sweet enough to sit in a browser
tab and be my primary reference.

A drawback of this simplicity is that parts of Zig can feel bare-bones
compared to more complex languages.  When I first adopted it, there was a
painful period where I yearned for the implicit type conversions and abstract
traits of other, more complex languages.  Zig promises to be a language where
code stays simple and concrete, but this becomes a stumbling block when you
find places where you would usually put abstraction.

What I found over time is that while Zig comes with very few built in type
mechanics, the first-class nature of its types allow you to borrow liberally
from other type systems without too much effort.  In doing so, it forces you to
think about what is really happening under the hood when you use a Java
interface or a Rust trait.

