# Polymorphism Out of the Box

This article is not meant to be a comprehensive intro to Zig interfaces.  For
that, see this *excellent* article by Karl Seguin: [Zig
Interfaces](https://www.openmymind.net/Zig-Interfaces/).  However as a starting
point, I think it's necessary to at least touch on what you get out of the box
with Zig.  These conventions become the foundation for the more theoretical
stuff that will follow.

## Comptime Duck Typing

If you learned Zig through any of the popular learning resources, you have
already been introduced to duck typing during comptime.  This idiom is used
broadly within the Zig standard library, and it is sufficient for many use
cases.

```zig
// FROM: https://ziglang.org/documentation/0.16.0/#Compile-Time-Parameters
fn max(comptime T: type, a: T, b: T) T {
    return if (a > b) a else b;
}
fn gimmeTheBiggerFloat(a: f32, b: f32) f32 {
    return max(f32, a, b);
}
fn gimmeTheBiggerInteger(a: u64, b: u64) u64 {
    return max(u64, a, b);
}
```

```zig
// FROM: https://ziglang.org/documentation/0.16.0/#Function-Parameter-Type-Inference
fn addFortyTwo(x: anytype) @TypeOf(x) {
    return x + 42;
}
```

Comptime duck typing is the practical, “default” way to accomplish static
polymorphism in Zig, and it is the simplest way to implement a lot of everyday
abstraction.  I’ve spent enough time writing Python to be wary of duck typing,
but restricting duck typing to comptime elegantly solves most of my concerns.
If type `T` in the above example is not comparable, or `x` is not a number, the
invocation will fail to compile.

It also makes it very easy to reason about the code that will be produced by the 

It is also trivially easy to validate input types at compile time.  Not only
does this help you explicitly check your invariants, but it naturally leads to
more legible error messages for the developer.

```zig
pub fn printName(entity: anytype) void {
    comptime if (!@hasField(@TypeOf(entity), "name")) {
        @compileError("entity must have a 'name' field.");
    };
    std.debug.print("Name: {s}\n", .{entity.name});
}

```

There are, however, some drawbacks to duck typing.  It makes it difficult to
encode type constraints on functions in a repeatable way, and this can be
cumbersome if you are used to type bounds such as interfaces, traits or
typeclasses.

### Comptime Example: ncast

## Runtime Interfaces


The Zig standard library also makes frequent use of fat pointers and vtables in
its `Allocator` and `IO` structs.  These are notable first because these serve
as a practical example of runtime polymorphism conventions in zig, and second
because they illustrate an important underlying principle:

> Zig makes you think about *how* type sugars in other languages work.

To see what I mean, let's look at an abridged version of the `Allocator`
interface and how the `ArenaAllocator` implementation exposes it.

```zig
const Allocator = struct {
    ptr: *anyopaque,
    vtable: *const VTable,

    pub const VTable = struct {
        alloc: *const fn (*anyopaque, len: usize, alignment: Alignment, ret_addr: usize) ?[*]u8,
        resize: *const fn (*anyopaque, memory: []u8, alignment: Alignment, new_len: usize, ret_addr: usize) bool,
        remap: *const fn (*anyopaque, memory: []u8, alignment: Alignment, new_len: usize, ret_addr: usize) ?[*]u8,
        free: *const fn (*anyopaque, memory: []u8, alignment: Alignment, ret_addr: usize) void,
    };

    // [...] (functions built on top of the vtable)
}

const ArenaAllocator = struct {
    child_allocator: Allocator,
    state: State,

    pub fn allocator(arena: *ArenaAllocator) Allocator {
        return .{
            .ptr = arena,
            .vtable = &.{
                .alloc = alloc,
                .resize = resize,
                .remap = remap,
                .free = free,
            },
        };
    }

    fn alloc(ctx: *anyopaque, n: usize, alignment: Alignment, ret_addr: usize) ?[*]u8 { ... }
    fn resize(*anyopaque, memory: []u8, alignment: Alignment, new_len: usize, ret_addr: usize) bool { ... }
    fn remap(*anyopaque, memory: []u8, alignment: Alignment, new_len: usize, ret_addr: usize) ?[*]u8 { ... }
    fn free(*anyopaque, memory: []u8, alignment: Alignment, ret_addr: usize) void { ... }

    // [...] arena-specific functions
}
```

I love this pattern.  It's an elegant representation of exactly how other
languages use dynamic dispatch under the hood to implement runtime
polymorphism.  However virtual function calls like these also haved drawbacks.
The opaque types combined with dynamic dispatch confound a lot of compiler
optimizations, as well as incurring a runtime overhead.

If you're clever, there are some good ways to minimize this overhead by keeping
the hot path outside of the dynamic dispatch. Take for example this (toy)
reader interface:

```zig
ptr: *anyopaque,
vtable: *const VTable,
buffer: [4096]u8,
head: usize,
tail: usize,

const VTable = struct {
    readBytes: *const fn (ptr: *anyopaque, dest: []u8) anyerror!usize,
}

fn readByte(self: *@This()) !u8 {
    if (self.head >= self.tail) {
        // slow path: read more bytes from interface
        @branchHint(.unlikely);
        self.tail = try self.vtable.readBytes(self.ptr, &self.buffer);
        self.head = 0;
        if (self.tail == 0) return error.end_of_stream;
    }

    // fast path: read buffered byte
    defer self.head += 1;
    return self.buffer[self.head];
}
```

This is pretty close to how Zig's current Reader and Writer interfaces are
implemented.  It's a little out of scope for this article, but for a deeper
dive see [this excellent
article](https://joegm.github.io/blog/inside-zigs-new-writer-interface/) by Joe
Mckay.

